course: medicine
lesson: 10
title: Tummy bugs and tummy aches
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A tummy bug, gastroenteritis, is an infection of the stomach and gut, often by norovirus; being sick and having diarrhoea (runny poo) is your body throwing the germs out.
explain: No medicine kills a tummy bug virus; small sips of water often, rest and a bowl nearby get you through in a few days.
explain: You can still pass it on for about two days after the last time you were sick, so the 48-hour rule keeps the whole class from catching it.
explain: Worry can really give you a tummy ache, because the brain and gut are wired together by nerves; it is real, not made up.
explain: Pain that starts round the belly button and moves to the bottom right side, with fever, can be appendicitis and needs a doctor.
fact: Gastroenteritis is an infection of the stomach and gut, usually by a virus such as norovirus, and the body clears it in a few days.
fact: Children can pass on a tummy bug for about 48 hours after the last vomit or diarrhoea, so they stay off school for that long.
fact: Dehydration means losing too much water and salts; signs are a dry mouth, dark or little wee, no tears, dizziness and tiredness.
fact: Oral rehydration sachets mix salts and sugar in water so the gut absorbs it well; fizzy drinks and fruit juice can make diarrhoea worse.
fact: A doctor is needed if a child can't keep fluids down, stops weeing, gets very drowsy or has blood in the poo.
fact: Worry and stress can cause real tummy aches because the brain and gut are connected by nerves.
fact: Appendicitis pain often starts round the belly button and moves to the lower right side, and it needs an operation.
fact: Food poisoning comes from germs in food not cooked through, not kept cool, or handled with dirty hands; food can look and smell fine and still have germs.
fact: Constipation means hard poo that is difficult to pass; water, fruit, vegetables and wholegrains help, and holding it in makes it worse.
fact: In coeliac disease the immune system, the body's defence against germs, attacks the gut lining when someone eats gluten, found in wheat, barley and rye; it needs a lifelong gluten-free diet.
fact: Studies show sugar does not make most children hyperactive (over-excited and unable to sit still), but too much at once can make you feel sick.
fact: After a tummy bug, simple food in small amounts is best; greasy food may upset the gut again.
exercise: What is gastroenteritis? | An infection of the stomach and gut | A stomach that has been broken by a fall | Eating your dinner far too fast | A kind of headache that moves to the tummy
exercise: What is the best thing to give a child with a tummy bug? | Small sips of water often | A big glass of cold fizzy drink | A large plate of hot salty chips | Nothing to drink at all until it stops
exercise: Why do you stay off school for 48 hours after you were last sick? | You can still pass the bug on | You need two days to catch up on all your sleep | The teacher says so just for fun | Your tummy needs two days of TV to settle
exercise: Which is a sign of dehydration? | Dark wee or hardly any wee | Crying lots and lots of tears | Feeling very full after a drink | Bright pink cheeks and cold hands
exercise: What is in an oral rehydration sachet? | Salts and sugar to mix with water | Antibiotics to kill the tummy bug | A big dose of vitamin C | Chocolate powder to make it taste nice
exercise: Your tummy hurts every morning before school. What could cause it? | Worry, since the brain and gut are linked | Your bed is too soft and squashes your tummy | The sun rising too early | Brushing your teeth too hard in the morning
exercise: Pain started at the belly button and moved to the bottom right side, with fever. What could it be? | Appendicitis, which needs a doctor | Just a bit of trapped wind | Growing pains in the tummy muscles | A pulled muscle from running
exercise: Why were you sick after the barbecue? | Germs in undercooked or badly kept food | The smoke got down into your tummy | Barbecue food is always a bit poisoned | You ran and played too much after eating
exercise: What helps constipation? | More water, fruit, vegetables and wholegrains | Holding the poo in for as long as you can until it softens | Eating only white bread and cheese | Drinking only fizzy drinks for a day
exercise: Your friend can't eat bread because of coeliac disease. What is it? | The immune system attacks the gut when gluten is eaten | A fussy fashion diet | An allergy to the butter spread on bread | A tummy bug he caught from bread once
exercise: Does sugar make most children hyperactive? | No, studies show it doesn't, but too much makes you feel sick | Yes, always, within a few minutes of eating even a little of it | Yes, but only on birthdays | No, sugar always makes you sleepy instead
exercise: After a tummy bug you want chips straight away. Good idea? | Better to start with simple food in small amounts | Yes, greasy salty food settles the gut down nicely | Yes, the more chips the better | No, you should never eat chips again
exercise: Is there a medicine that kills a tummy bug virus? | No, the body clears it in a few days | Yes, antibiotics kill it in a day | Yes, cough syrup does | Yes, vitamin tablets taken with water
exercise: When does a child with a tummy bug need a doctor? | If he can't keep fluids down or stops weeing | If he is sick just once in the night | If he wants to watch TV all day | If he is bored of lying in bed
exercise: Can food look and smell fine and still give you food poisoning? | Yes, germs can be invisible | No, bad food always smells bad | No, only green food is bad | Yes, but only ever fish and prawns
test: puked all night, feel ok now, can I go in today? | Not yet; wait 48 hours after the last time you were sick | Yeah, feeling OK means you're not catching any more, so go | Yeah, if you wash your hands once | No, stay off for a whole month
test: Mum says flat cola is good for a tummy bug. What's better? | Oral rehydration solution or small sips of water | Lots of sweet fruit juice | Energy drinks to get your strength back | A thick milkshake to line the tummy
test: Leo says his morning tummy aches are fake because the doctor found nothing wrong. Right? | No, worry can cause real tummy pain | Yes, he's just making it up | Yes, real tummy aches always have germs | No, it means he's got appendicitis
test: In a cartoon a dog eats a whole cake and is sick but is fixed with one pill. What's wrong about bugs and pills? | Most tummy upsets just need time, fluids and rest | Nothing, there is a pill for every tummy bug | Dogs can't be sick | Cake can't upset a tummy, only bad meat can
test: What would happen if the cook didn't wash her hands before making sandwiches? | Germs could spread into the food | The sandwiches would taste better | Nothing at all would happen | The bread would go stale much faster
test: My mate reckons people with coeliac disease are just being fussy. What do you say? | No, gluten really damages their gut | Yes, it's just a fashion diet | Yes, they just hate the taste of bread | No, coeliac means you love bread too much
test: Sam had a tummy bug, has a dry mouth, no tears when crying and is dizzy. What is happening? | He may be dehydrated | He is getting better fast | He's just hungry for his tea | He has hay fever on top
test: I've been holding in my poo all day at school because I hate the toilets. Any harm? | It can make constipation worse | No, holding it in is healthy | No, the poo just disappears | Yes, it gives you flu by the evening
test: At the party, Josh ate loads of sweets and his mum said "now he'll go crazy". What do studies say? | Sugar doesn't make most kids hyperactive | She's right, sugar always causes it | Sugar makes kids fall asleep straight away | Only red sweets do that, because of the colour
test: Why might a doctor want a scan for a tummy pain in one spot that's getting worse with fever? | It could be appendicitis | It's always just trapped wind | To find any sweets he swallowed | Because tummies are see-through on a scan
test: If a tummy bug virus can't be killed with medicine, how do people get better? | The body's own defences clear it in a few days | They don't, it lasts for ever | The sick bowl soaks the virus up | It slowly turns into an ordinary cold
exam: Identify the principal measure in the management of a child with gastroenteritis. | Frequent small quantities of fluid to prevent dehydration | Administration of antibiotics at the earliest opportunity | Abstention from all fluids | Provision of fizzy drinks in quantity
exam: It is stipulated that a child remain absent from school for forty-eight hours following the last episode of vomiting. State the reason. | The child may yet transmit the infection to others | The child requires additional sleep after the night's illness | The school must be cleaned for two days | It is an arbitrary disciplinary measure
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises appendicitis? | Inflammation of the appendix, with pain moving to the lower right abdomen | A disorder caused by worry alone | A form of food poisoning confined to the lower left abdomen | A consequence of the consumption of sweets
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning coeliac disease. | An immune reaction to gluten, managed by a lifelong gluten-free diet | A preference adopted for reasons of fashion | An infection caught from contaminated bread | A condition cured by a short course of tablets taken for one week
